      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Society for Vascular Surgery. Published by Mosby, Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Antegrade puncture is a routinely used technique of obtaining access to the common femoral artery to perform infrainguinal interventions. However, antegrade arterial access can be challenging in the presence of hostile, scarred groins, obesity, or a high common femoral artery bifurcation. A simple method of converting a retrograde femoral access to an antegrade catheterization using an inexpensive and universally available monofilament suture is presented."
      },
